WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.640
From its feverish inspiration to strange early
casting discussions to an iconic shot that

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.970
almost got the crew in trouble with the law,
here are a few facts you might not have known

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.000
about the making of The Terminator.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.960
Some films begin with a story, while others
begin with an image.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.660
In the case of The Terminator, it began with
a literal fever dream.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.580
After an awful experience filming Piranha
II, writer-director James Cameron retreated

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.980
to Rome to recover.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.560
He told Entertainment Weekly,

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.039
"Nightmares are a business asset; that's the
way I look at it.

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:35.690
I was sick, I was broke, I had a high fever,
and I had a dream about this metal death figure

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.820
coming out of a fire.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.540
And the implication was that it had been stripped
of its skin by the fire and exposed for what

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.580
it really was.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.280
When I have some particularly vivid image,
I'll draw it or I'll write some notes, and

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.580
that goes on to this day."

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.720
Cameron sketched his fever vision and eventually
showed the images to young producer Gale Anne

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:54.220
Hurd.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:55.580
The Terminator was born.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.380
With the idea set in their minds, Cameron
and Hurd collaborated on the film's script,

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.079
then began to shop it around to studios as
a low-budget, guerrilla-style film that Cameron

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.740
would direct and Hurd would produce.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:11.760
To keep costs low, they decided to have Lance
Henriksen play The Terminator, but when Orion

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.220
Pictures came on board with financing, they
had a much crazier idea: O.J. Simpson!

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.240
Cameron said no to that idea, but reluctantly
agreed to take a meeting with Orion head Mike

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.880
Medavoy's top choice to play the heroic time
traveler Kyle Reese.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.220
Medavoy's choice?

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.940
Conan the Barbarian star Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.219
Cameron was skeptical, but came away impressed
by Schwarzenegger's ideas for the character

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.120
of The Terminator.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.649
Instead of offering him the role of Reese,
Cameron offered Schwarzenegger the role of

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:40.280
Terminator.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.880
Schwarzenegger initially feared playing the
villain would hurt his leading man image,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.180
but Cameron won him over.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.260
Schwarzenegger told Entertainment Weekly,

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.299
"But Cameron said that he'd shoot it in such
a way that all the evil stuff that I do will

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.640
be totally excused by audiences because I'm
a cool machine.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.360
And so cool that some of the people will cheer."

00:01:58.720 --> 00:01:59.720
Schwarzenegger took the role.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.580
It would prove to be the best decision of
his career.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.320
He wasn't the only second pick in the film,
though.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:09.890
With Schwarzenegger off the board, numerous
stars were considered for the role of Kyle

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.140
Reese, including everyone from Mel Gibson
and Kurt Russell to Bruce Springsteen.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.300
Superman star Christopher Reeve declined a
million dollar offer to play the role, but

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.020
perhaps the strangest star to pass on the
film was the musician Sting.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.400
After being offered a cool $350,000 to play
the role, Sting ultimately passed because

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:32.900
of prior obligations including a role in the
epic science fiction flop Dune opening the

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:38.900
door for Michael Biehn, who would team with
Cameron again for Aliens in 1986 and The Abyss

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:39.400
in 1989.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.800
Even some of the talent behind the scenes
were second choices.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.680
Cameron and Hurd originally wanted legendary
makeup artist Dick Smith to design The Terminator's

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.180
look.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:53.100
He said no, but pointed them towards another
rising star in the business: Stan Winston,

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.890
who would go on to win four Academy Awards
for his effects work, including two for Terminator

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:58.380
2.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.620
Though she would go on to become an action
badass in subsequent films, in the first Terminator

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.240
movie, Sarah Connor spends the whole movie
running away.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.860
And that was a problem for star Linda Hamilton,
because just before shooting, she broke her

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.740
ankle, tearing some ligaments, too, just for
good measure.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.870
To make things easier on Hamilton, Hurd shuffled
the production schedule around so that many

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.600
of the scenes that involved running were shot
near the end of principal photography.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.440
But it was still far from easy.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.020
Hurd told Entertainment Weekly,

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:33.219
"She had to run throughout the movie, and
it was mostly Linda by the way, on a broken

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.340
ankle that had to be wrapped every day."

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.420
The Terminator franchise has endured for more
than three decades, and through it all one

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.610
line has remained a signature not just for
the franchise, but for Schwarzenegger as a

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:45.880
major action star:

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.660
"I'll be back."

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.840
Ironically, Schwarzenegger tried to change
the line.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:58.549
"I could not pronounce really well this 'I'll'
so I kept just saying to Jim Cameron, maybe

00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:01.120
I should just say 'I will be back'"

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.540
Cameron refused to budge.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.180
"He says, 'no, no, no.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:06.890
Look, I don't tell you how to act, don't tell
me how to write.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.060
I wrote the line, 'I'll be back.'"

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.940
Cameron shot several takes of the scene and
had Schwarzenegger deliver the same line a

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.540
different way each time, then picked the best
take.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.240
Almost immediately after the film was released,
fans were approaching Schwarzenegger on the

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:21.900
street to ask him to repeat it.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.160
Cameron and Hurd shot the film guerilla style,
filming many scenes without location permits.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.100
And that almost got the production in hot
water with the law during the filming of the

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.120
final scene when a cop crashed the party.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.060
Hurd told Entertainment Weekly,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.940
"[He] said, 'I need to see your permit for
filming here.'

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.160
And we didn't have one.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.460
We said, 'Oh, officer, we're making a UCLA
student film.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:44.420
We didn't know you needed permits.'

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.470
And he said, 'Okay, you're fine, just take
the camera off the road.'

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.360
I don't know if that cop ever figured it out.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.100
Wherever he is, we thank him for not shutting
us down."

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:57.560
In the summer of 1984, as The Terminator was
nearing completion, Cameron took a rough cut

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.060
to show the bosses at Orion.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.840
They were less than impressed, with Cameron
calling the screening, quote, "disastrous."

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.680
Hurd told Entertainment Weekly,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.620
"They had such little faith in the movie that
they didn't want to screen it for critics.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.740
The head of marketing almost said as much.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.360
And if you were Orion and you had Amadeus,
which they had released five weeks before,

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.620
and which did go on to win Best Picture, well,
I can imagine them saying, 'Amadeus, The Terminator

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.670
which one doesn't fit?'

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.120
I can't blame them now, but at the time I
was devastated when they didn't like it."

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.980
In The Terminator's closing credits, there's
one credit that may seem odd to viewers not

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.700
familiar with how it came about.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.540
It reads: "Acknowledgement to the works of
Harlan Ellison."

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:40.400
Ellison, of course, was a legendary science
fiction author who was almost as legendary

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.500
for being cantankerous.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:45.830
So when he discovered the similarities between
The Terminator and two Ellison's stories for

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:51.360
the 60's TV series Outer Limits, "Soldier"
and "Demon with a Glass Hand," he contacted

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.360
his lawyers.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.530
"We've been altered to look like humans.

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:56.720
It was necessary to come back into time and
find you."

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:58.480
"What do you want with me?"

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.260
"You're the last hope of Earth."

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:06.479
Cameron publicly denied ripping off Ellison's
stories, but Ellison reached a settlement

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.200
out of court with the studio that included
both money and that credit.

